      "text": "🧠 Understanding the Human Brain\n\nLesson 1 — Your brain, explained from the beginning\n\nYour brain is one of the most complex things we know of in the universe. It controls your thoughts, emotions, memories, movements, dreams, and even things you don't consciously think about, like breathing.\n\nAnd here's something fascinating: You're using your brain to learn about your brain right now.\n\nLet's explore how it works.\n\n1. What does the brain actually look like?\n11\n\nAn adult human brain typically weighs around 3 pounds (1.4 kilograms). It contains roughly 86 billion neurons, specialized cells that help transmit information.\n\nYour brain has three major anatomical regions:\n\nCerebrum: The largest part, involved in thinking, memory, perception, language, and voluntary movement.\n\nCerebellum: Helps coordinate movement, balance, timing, and motor learning.\n\nBrainstem: Helps regulate vital functions such as breathing, heart rate, and wakefulness.\n\n2. The four lobes of the brain\nposterazzi.com\n\nYour cerebral cortex is divided into four major lobes on each side.\n\nFrontal lobe — Your planner\n\nHelps with decision-making, attention, impulse control, personality, and voluntary movement.\n\nParietal lobe — Your body and space processor\n\nProcesses touch and body sensations and helps you understand where things are in space.\n\nTemporal lobe — Sound, language, and memory\n\nHelps you understand speech, recognize sounds, and form memories.\n\nOccipital lobe — Your visual processor\n\nProcesses information from your eyes, helping you recognize shapes, colors, and movement.\n\nThese regions work together constantly. A single thought or action usually involves several brain areas.\n\n3. How do brain cells communicate?\nconceptviz.app\n\nYour brain contains billions of nerve cells called neurons.\n\nThink of neurons as tiny messengers.\n\nHere's how communication happens:\n\nA neuron receives signals through its dendrites.\n\nIt integrates those signals and may generate an electrical impulse.\n\nThe impulse travels down its axon.\n\nAt many synapses, chemical messengers called neurotransmitters carry the signal to another cell.\n\nSome important neurotransmitters include:\n\nChemical\n\n\t\n\nImportant roles\n\n\n\n\nDopamine\n\n\t\n\nMotivation, learning, movement, reward\n\n\n\n\nSerotonin\n\n\t\n\nMood regulation, sleep, appetite\n\n\n\n\nGABA\n\n\t\n\nReduces neural activity\n\n\n\n\nGlutamate\n\n\t\n\nExcitatory signaling, learning, memory\n\n\n\n\nAcetylcholine\n\n\t\n\nAttention, memory, muscle activation\n\nOne surprising fact: dopamine isn't simply a happiness chemical. It also helps your brain learn from unexpected outcomes and decide which actions are worth repeating.\n\n4. How does your brain create memories?\n\nTwo important structures are:\n\nHippocampus\n\nEssential for forming many new memories of events and experiences.\n\nAmygdala\n\nHelps process emotional significance, especially threats and emotionally important experiences.\n\nImagine meeting someone new.\n\nYour brain processes their face, voice, name, and the situation. The hippocampus helps bind aspect",
