      "message": 2,
      "state": "state_1",
      "statesRecorded": 2,
      "text": "Yes! 🧠 All four lobes of your brain can work at the same time. In fact, they are constantly communicating with each other, even when you're doing something simple.\n\nBut here's the interesting part: they don't all work equally hard at every moment. Different activities require different brain regions to become more active.\n\nExample: Imagine you're eating pizza 🍕\n\n1. Frontal lobe\n\nYou decide to pick up another slice and control your hand movements.\n\n2. Parietal lobe\n\nYou feel the pizza's temperature and texture and sense where your hand is.\n\n3. Temporal lobe\n\nYou hear your friend talking and understand what they're saying.\n\n4. Occipital lobe\n\nYou see the pizza's shape, colors, and movement as you lift it.\n\nAll four lobes can be doing these jobs simultaneously, along with other brain structures!\n\nThink of your brain like an orchestra 🎻\n\nEach lobe is like a different group of instruments. They have different roles, but they work together to create one coordinated performance.\n\nYour brain doesn't need to finish seeing something before it starts thinking about it. Many processes happen in parallel.\n\nAnd even when you're asleep, activity continues across all four lobes, although the patterns change.\n\nQuick question for you!\n\nIf you're watching a movie and suddenly hear a loud explosion, which lobes help you see the explosion and hear the sound?\n\nOnly the frontal lobe\nOccipital and temporal lobes\nOnly the parietal lobe\n\n Correct! The occipital lobe processes visual information, while the temporal lobe processes sound. They work together.\n\nOne more fascinating thing: Your brain doesn't have just one control center directing everything. Many networks cooperate and influence one another. That's part of what makes the human brain so remarkable.",
